<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=anden">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andén</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">by 2000 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Andenes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An andén (plural andenes) is a stair-step terrace dug into a hillside for farming, built by pre-Columbian cultures in the Andes by 2000 BCE. Layers of stone, gravel and topsoil behind stone retaining walls drained the soil, while the walls stored the sun's heat against frost; one aim was to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> up to 3,500 metres. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> andenes covered an estimated 1,000,000 hectares, and many are still farmed after more than 500 years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anden.jpg" alt="Andén" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: www.janto.com.ar · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pisaq_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 2000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>The Andes</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The origin of terracing in the Andes is poorly understood.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/potato">Potato<span>8000–5000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=anden">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
